fglm -- convert a Groebner basis
********************************

  * Usage: 
        H = fglm(G, R)
        H = fglm(I, R)
  * Inputs:
      * G, a *note Gröbner basis: (Macaulay2Doc)GroebnerBasis,, the starting
        Groebner basis
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the starting ideal
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, a ring with the target monomial
        order
  * Outputs:
      * H, a *note Gröbner basis: (Macaulay2Doc)GroebnerBasis,, the new
        Groebner basis in the target monomial order

Description
===========

FGLM takes a Groebner basis of an ideal with respect to one monomial order and
changes it into a Groebner basis of the same ideal over a different monomial
order. The initial order is given by the ring of G and the target order is the
order in R. When given an ideal I as input a Groebner basis of I in the ring of
I is initially computed directly, and then this Groebner basis is converted
into a Groebner basis in the ring R.

+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R1 = QQ[x,y,z];                                                        |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I1 = ideal(x^2 + 2*y^2 - y - 2*z, x^2 - 8*y^2 + 10*z - 1, x^2 - 7*y*z);|
|                                                                            |
|o2 : Ideal of R1                                                            |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : R2 = QQ[x,y,z, MonomialOrder => Lex];                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : fglm(I1, R2)                                                           |
|                                                                            |
|o4 = GroebnerBasis[status: done; S-pairs encountered up to degree 0]        |
|                                                                            |
|o4 : GroebnerBasis                                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The ideal I generated by G must be zero-dimensional. The target ring R must be
the same ring as the ring of G or I, except with different monomial order. R
must be a polynomial ring over a field.

